#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133884 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133700 :question/id #uuid "3ff94d55-a99a-5de6-bc84-410d03f18a28" :question/math-academy-id "e-9086" :question/problem "Consider the triangle $C$ in the plane with vertices $(0,0), (1,1)$ and $(-1,1),$ traversed counterclockwise, and the region $D$ bounded by $C.$ Express $\\displaystyle{\\oint\\limits_{C}x\\sin{y}\\,\\mathrm{d}x+ (x^2\\cos{y}-y^2)\\,\\mathrm{d}y}$ as a repeated integral." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} P\\,\\mathrm{d}x + Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y \n= \\iint\\limits_D \\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\textrm d A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/2f/2fa3c84359cec5f5e0750552e5a4b24b20b8dbc3d65b909aea908e1505cfb5e3.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)= x\\sin{y}$ and $Q(x,y)= x^2\\cos{y}-y^2.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nD = \\{(x,y) :  -y\\leq x\\leq y,\\, 0\\leq y \\leq 1\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,dx+({x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-y}^{y}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "48652211-e469-55b9-96cb-2526a4135d34" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049997 :topic/math-academy-id 2151 :topic/title "Double Integrals Over Type I Regions"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050264 :topic/math-academy-id 2152 :topic/title "Double Integrals Over Type II Regions"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134068 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24768801-8fab-53fd-a0cd-39980d484a21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2x}2xy\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241496} {:answer/id #uuid "431267f1-e896-500c-84d8-2cf2426b2403" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2x}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241781} {:answer/id #uuid "991fc2be-8a77-504e-b717-422b1852dda6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2x}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242518} {:answer/id #uuid "9fedb320-619d-5fd4-bb97-8324e0b2afd7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2x}x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242580} {:answer/id #uuid "b8be4494-9828-596a-a764-bd23250e5578" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{x}2x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242800}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "24768801-8fab-53fd-a0cd-39980d484a21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2x}2xy\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241496} :answer-field/id #uuid "f91572ca-da1d-5596-9c9c-7fe17a660ab0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243874}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "700fbfa1-78ed-5b39-a539-ef3e4dd8874d" :question/math-academy-id "q-108467" :question/problem "Consider the triangle $C$ in the plane with vertices $(0,0),(1,0)$ and $(1,2),$ traversed counterclockwise, and the region $D$ bounded by $C.$ Express ${∮}_{C}x{y}^{2}\\,dx+2{x}^{2}y\\,dy$ as a repeated integral." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/2e/2e63a57e50b60d7f0c6a3ce12d164716e2cba71c6bd4c6df10460a35beba4209.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=x{y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)=2{x}^{2}y.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2xy,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=4xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):0\\le x\\le 1,0\\le y\\le 2x}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}x{y}^{2}\\,dx+2{x}^{2}y\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(4xy-2xy)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2x}2xy\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134375 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03eefc0e-dc5f-50f8-8aa8-37fedc5b6795" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}({x}^{2}-3y)\\text{ }dy\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415777} {:answer/id #uuid "4dfb517d-314f-5d0e-adde-8ffca8af7876" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}y(2x-3)\\text{ }dy\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415796} {:answer/id #uuid "d8a94d74-4321-5c41-9daa-93ace7652c67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}x(3y+2)\\text{ }dy\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415835} {:answer/id #uuid "e3efbf16-6539-5ad4-bd13-0040d7d457e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}x(2y-3)\\text{ }dy\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415838} {:answer/id #uuid "eb384a4b-9a06-5ed5-bfb1-8be217c6cefa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}x(3y-2)\\text{ }dy\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415839}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e3efbf16-6539-5ad4-bd13-0040d7d457e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}x(2y-3)\\text{ }dy\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415838} :answer-field/id #uuid "9db11add-d8be-5410-9153-1e6c3098e557" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415851}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1939a88a-677c-5293-8524-d0ecfaa79350" :question/math-academy-id "q-42085" :question/problem "Consider the square $C$ in the plane with vertices $(-1,0),(2,0),(2,3)$ and $(-1,3),$ traversed counterclockwise, and the region $D$ bounded by $C.$ Express ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr,$  where $F(x,y)=⟨3xy,{x}^{2}y⟩,$ as a repeated integral." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/6f/6f831b2b707e63e8eb34ff5f6c1c45d0267f51001de2560834d212506b48ee41.png)\n\nFirst, notice that we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}3xy\\,dx+{x}^{2}y\\,dy\n$$\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=3xy$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{2}y.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=3x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 3\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}3xy\\,dx+{x}^{2}y\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2xy-3x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}x(2y-3)\\text{ }dy\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244254 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c64f97f-3cc5-550d-9b53-b55d5beece33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{y}^{4y-{y}^{2}}({e}^{y}-2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241286} {:answer/id #uuid "10fe1edc-f3c0-53d9-ba62-f20627efd921" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y}^{4y-{y}^{2}}x{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241328} {:answer/id #uuid "6a3eb839-93ab-5ade-a75d-b2a6f9373bea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{y}^{4y-{y}^{2}}(2x{e}^{y}-1)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242135} {:answer/id #uuid "c510257b-72ce-50c4-8778-b5bdef960fb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y}^{3y-{y}^{2}}{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242916} {:answer/id #uuid "dca979b3-434a-561b-8d4a-39e12feb7842" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{y}^{4y-{y}^{2}}{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243143}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dca979b3-434a-561b-8d4a-39e12feb7842" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{y}^{4y-{y}^{2}}{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243143} :answer-field/id #uuid "f269e409-7bf4-5c2e-903d-d263915d4aba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243857}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "806c47fd-68e8-5b88-97f5-76f2521ac858" :question/math-academy-id "q-109249" :question/problem "Consider the closed curve $C,$ which is the boundary of the region enclosed by the curves $x=y$ and $x=4y-{y}^{2}.$ Express ${∮}_{C}({e}^{y}-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\,dx+(2x{e}^{y}+{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,dy$ as a repeated integral." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n \n\n![](images/23/239867aec8cff7840667d2f4eafa9bc97d4fc5af09b684d863fef06fd8fd28ad.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={e}^{y}-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$ and $Q(x,y)=2x{e}^{y}+{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}={e}^{y}+2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2{e}^{y}+2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):y\\le x\\le 4y-{y}^{2},\\,0\\le y\\le 3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({e}^{y}-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\,dx+(2x{e}^{y}+{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2{e}^{y}+2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-{e}^{y}-2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{y}^{4y-{y}^{2}}{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244257 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6c6fb1e6-13c2-5304-9a0b-fd9f38873188" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{x+2}2(x+y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242152} {:answer/id #uuid "8855dd4e-cf52-5421-a738-17951cb4daa8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{x+2}^{{x}^{2}+x+2}2(x+y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242377} {:answer/id #uuid "c441ba1b-5791-5a9d-88bf-8622cc73ba19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-{x}^{2}}^{x+2}2(x+y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242905} {:answer/id #uuid "cc6ea074-1c33-5438-b183-744a1d23db35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{x+2}2(x-y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242975} {:answer/id #uuid "d5d53892-c143-515e-b312-92905c82dc37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{x+2}(2y-x)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243072}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6c6fb1e6-13c2-5304-9a0b-fd9f38873188" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{x+2}2(x+y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242152} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a6164af-b887-57b9-91dd-6c4ddd92fdb5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243488}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a5b7043e-fa21-5314-98d6-598e5c27911d" :question/math-academy-id "q-109263" :question/problem "Consider the closed curve $C,$ which is the boundary of the region enclosed by the curves $y={x}^{2}$ and $y=x+2.$ Express ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr,$  where $F(x,y)=⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+x)-{y}^{2},{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}⟩,$ as a repeated integral." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n \n\n![](images/e3/e32b8e7d6ce80d7cedad928f9a2f7acc3c3d7f06add7909e856db27d154068d6.png)\n\nFirst, notice that we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+x)-{y}^{2})\\,dx+({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,dy\n$$\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+x)-{y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-2y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 2,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le x+2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)-{y}^{2})\\,dx+({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2x+2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{x+2}2(x+y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326906 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "003753be-91cd-5401-b185-1a2caa7b0ba9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}^{0}x\\,dy\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323978} {:answer/id #uuid "11403fb2-cc40-5faf-bc1a-820a3836d9e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}(x+y)dy\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324129} {:answer/id #uuid "14390247-b8d4-5256-ae63-0dceb79e5d7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}^{0}dy\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324154} {:answer/id #uuid "acbb3d66-c472-5d9a-9faa-95357fdcbd7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}^{0}(x-y)dy\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325437} {:answer/id #uuid "f6e9f50f-53fb-5ee2-baa4-64890b3e25c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}^{0}dy\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326148}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f6e9f50f-53fb-5ee2-baa4-64890b3e25c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}^{0}dy\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326148} :answer-field/id #uuid "c166fb2a-6179-53b7-8884-9c52889f51d0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326570}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dfeb76a7-b3b4-5da5-8979-e2b8f3b5d4af" :question/math-academy-id "q-41702" :question/problem "Consider the closed curve $C,$ which is the boundary of the region enclosed by $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and the $x$-axis, for $\\pi \\le x\\le 2\\pi .$ Express ${\\int }_{C}xdx-(x-y)dy$ as a repeated integral." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/26/264cb888010525848f647669653d34383535dd32e6ac264927105540c3b85f9f.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=x$ and $Q(x,y)=-(x-y).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):\\pi \\le x\\le 2\\pi ,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\le y\\le 0}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the Green's Theorem we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}xdx-(x-y)dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-1)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}^{0}dy\\,dx.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326928 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "019501e8-6ea6-5368-99e8-cde9c215f7d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{y+1}(1-x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\text{ }dx\\,dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323987} {:answer/id #uuid "31bcb739-40d2-561c-b2d4-44bd167631a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{y}(1+x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\text{ }dx\\,dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324375} {:answer/id #uuid "6f3759c8-4733-51a4-abea-b8fa6dcbec97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{y+1}(1+x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\text{ }dx\\,dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324885} {:answer/id #uuid "b9f9d8ec-7102-5b9e-a9d0-b789d1dc61fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{y}(1+x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\text{ }dx\\,dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325569} {:answer/id #uuid "de42923d-c758-5be4-a046-273bd9c961bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{y+1}(1+x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\text{ }dx\\,dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325911}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6f3759c8-4733-51a4-abea-b8fa6dcbec97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{y+1}(1+x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\text{ }dx\\,dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324885} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e66e1ab-6bec-5d89-bae1-cf35e17f31ed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326348}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "977cc01a-2db6-51fc-a3f5-772fb936cc60" :question/math-academy-id "q-42107" :question/problem "Consider the triangle $C$ in the plane with vertices $(0,0),(0,-1)$ and $(1,0),$ and the region $D$ bounded by $C.$ Express ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr,$  where $F(x,y)=⟨x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y⟩,$ as a repeated integral." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/6d/6d4f13abad5cc193dccedee3114fbd9cbd9fb8e6b5b3ddb0d07f0ed2ed51f4ff.png)\n\nFirst, notice that we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,dx+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,dy\n$$\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$ and $Q(x,y)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):0\\le x\\le y+1,\\,-1\\le y\\le 0}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,dx+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{y+1}(1+x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\text{ }dx\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362505 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19d912c2-2ff0-503a-91db-848ccbab9dca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362507}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "19d912c2-2ff0-503a-91db-848ccbab9dca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362507} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9a9ee48-7978-5cd0-a274-d255560a0858" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362506}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "076b553d-9b94-5414-af96-ed5daed9cd1c" :question/math-academy-id "q-168558" :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to write down a double integral that gives the circulation of $F$ along $C,$ transversed counterclockwise.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/46/4693916207540a6453c6c3775824ba577e62fea0a18b07e96f8814901d9ad80e.png)\n\nFirst, notice that we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}({x}^{2}-2{y}^{2})\\,dx+(-2xy)\\,dy\n$$\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)=-2xy.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-4y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-2y-(-4y))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}2y\\,dy\\,dx.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386390 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e823e3a1-e75b-5988-a51e-22e31b8a9028" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}(1+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386392} {:answer/id #uuid "fcde3246-eebb-595d-9c9f-b55275d0c8a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}^{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386393} {:answer/id #uuid "8f60dcb7-b93b-52d6-ab98-048f7b9af687" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}^{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386394} {:answer/id #uuid "c8832ecf-0003-515c-96de-a76fba6e0484" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386395} {:answer/id #uuid "76ced2c3-93fa-57fb-804a-79e1c5bb7e94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386396}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "76ced2c3-93fa-57fb-804a-79e1c5bb7e94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386396} :answer-field/id #uuid "375112e1-4816-53a5-b797-a9ab4149ddab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386391}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ec0b1748-e212-5d96-8358-0b705e724a9f" :question/math-academy-id "q-109254" :question/problem "Consider the closed curve $C,$ which is the boundary of the region enclosed by the unit circle and the coordinate axes in the first quadrant. Express ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr,$  where $F(x,y)=⟨y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,x-{e}^{-{y}^{2}}⟩,$ as a repeated integral." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n \n\n![](images/e4/e48034aed1f41cf1f9b30c057186879233fe05cd2ba720dcee436398a732b6ce.png)\n\nFirst, notice that we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,dx+(x-{e}^{-{y}^{2}})\\,dy\n$$\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and $Q(x,y)=x-{e}^{-{y}^{2}}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,dx+(x-{e}^{-{y}^{2}})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386397 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c0ab6a5-fedc-5172-9726-7c8b92e6d238" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{-2x+2}y\\text{ }dy\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386399} {:answer/id #uuid "269ba7b5-dbe7-55bd-823b-41e9e736d2f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x-1}^{-2x+2}(y+4y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\text{ }dy\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386400} {:answer/id #uuid "70a164ad-574b-5019-8963-919944f5cbe9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x-1}^{-2x+2}y\\text{ }dy\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386401} {:answer/id #uuid "985a602a-628b-5bc3-b3e1-54bc6f12da7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{x+1}^{-2x+2}y\\text{ }dy\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386402} {:answer/id #uuid "215ea562-e0fc-5144-bcf0-a8d1cb3bb25f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x-1}^{-2x+2}(x-(2+{y}^{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\text{ }dy\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386403}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "70a164ad-574b-5019-8963-919944f5cbe9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x-1}^{-2x+2}y\\text{ }dy\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386401} :answer-field/id #uuid "68b205c3-aadd-5002-89e3-15ef39ecf484" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386398}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d02b88c9-898d-50c4-a59e-b0f309ffc0a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-109291" :question/problem "Consider the triangle $C$ in the plane with vertices $(0,-1),(1,0)$ and $(0,2),$ and the region $D$ bounded by $C.$ Express ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr,$  where $F(x,y)=⟨{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,xy-2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x⟩,$ as a repeated integral." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/c2/c2e08c233cb31124234ab6e5179998a3bdc148024632c7770bdea46cd37dff83.png)\n\nFirst, notice that we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,dx+(xy-2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,dy\n$$\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $Q(x,y)=xy-2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=y+2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):0\\le x\\le 1,\\,x-1\\le y\\le -2x+2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,dx+(xy-2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(y+2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x-1}^{-2x+2}y\\text{ }dy\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415860 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "268fff15-9ff4-545d-b493-583e83e9bc8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+2y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415784} {:answer/id #uuid "97cc6eca-3853-56fc-b7f2-30027a479f07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+x)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415817} {:answer/id #uuid "9947a74c-b42b-54c1-b91a-071c2ba392f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{2}^{1+x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-2y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415818} {:answer/id #uuid "bb803066-80a1-53e9-86c1-b29e905214f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{2}^{1+x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+x)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415825} {:answer/id #uuid "f479d213-1c2b-52df-b690-7c60433780fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-2y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415841}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f479d213-1c2b-52df-b690-7c60433780fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-2y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415841} :answer-field/id #uuid "546404cf-f1c6-53f4-be84-033edffb255a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415847}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0fd75856-9255-5679-88e6-6aeda24c9a6c" :question/math-academy-id "q-109288" :question/problem "Consider the triangle $C$ in the plane with vertices $(-1,0),(1,0)$ and $(-1,2),$ and the region $D$ bounded by $C.$ Express ${∮}_{C}({y}^{2}-2xy)\\,dx+(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-{x}^{2})\\,dy$ as a repeated integral." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/ea/eac7a442f0a794db0606b95aa722320d099f2bcc4e530fc6b62e9be889335da2.png)\n\n \n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={y}^{2}-2xy$ and $Q(x,y)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-{x}^{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2y-2x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1-x\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({y}^{2}-2xy)\\,dx+(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-{x}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-2x-(2y-2x)]\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-2y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415861 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ea792f4-c1dd-5faa-a83b-af57c9e0e101" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}}2xy\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415792} {:answer/id #uuid "89af553e-248b-5c72-ab62-1d4e4315eda8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1+{y}^{2}}}(2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+{e}^{x})\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415812} {:answer/id #uuid "d8db4f17-8974-53ec-bf38-95643619fcaf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415836} {:answer/id #uuid "e1b0ae0b-92be-5756-b5b7-435352928f96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}}^{\\sqrt{1+{y}^{2}}}2xy\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415837} {:answer/id #uuid "f8bad17e-7a80-55fa-877b-8cf3e386ec15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}}(2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+{e}^{x})\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415843}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3ea792f4-c1dd-5faa-a83b-af57c9e0e101" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}}2xy\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415792} :answer-field/id #uuid "55995149-0733-5aaa-8feb-5219bcb91ca4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415848}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "196f72ff-1ff2-5e8e-8905-6141cb15ad66" :question/math-academy-id "q-109248" :question/problem "Consider the closed curve $C,$ which is the boundary of the region enclosed by the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ and the $y$-axis, for $x\\ge 0.$ Express ${∮}_{C}(x{y}^{2}-{e}^{x})\\,dx+(2{x}^{2}y-1)\\,dy$ as a repeated integral." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/92/92b9a6c83dd18a5599bbee71d714b26fddd62eb32917a5df983edc232664067b.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=x{y}^{2}-{e}^{x}$ and $Q(x,y)=2{x}^{2}y-1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2xy,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=4xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\le x\\le \\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}},\\,-1\\le y\\le 1\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(x{y}^{2}-{e}^{x})\\,dx+(2{x}^{2}y-1)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(4xy-2xy)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2xy\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}}2xy\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415870 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05110615-25ea-51a7-9ec6-fe0602b9d168" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(y+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415778} {:answer/id #uuid "1f28acf4-a8d0-5ea3-bf43-bdd1494c0d0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-2}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}y(2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415782} {:answer/id #uuid "50d8a1be-eef6-58bc-8c04-9f0c7278b337" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-2}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(y+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415797} {:answer/id #uuid "6622f567-4548-58c5-9664-16e371447a8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-2}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415804} {:answer/id #uuid "d3af5033-176c-54c5-aa13-e691e816dd84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415832}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6622f567-4548-58c5-9664-16e371447a8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-2}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415804} :answer-field/id #uuid "0551862e-7b6d-55d9-92c8-f74ab0aa1c10" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415846}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c14fef09-0118-528c-b9c9-91bf74ebf7eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-109245" :question/problem "Consider the rectangle $C$ in the plane with vertices $(-2,0),(1,0),(1,1)$ and $(-2,1),$ and the region $D$ bounded by $C.$ Express ${∮}_{C}y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,dx+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+x{y}^{2})\\,dy$ as a repeated integral." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/a5/a5404053d83188af931c19350d5b6e42145009cf7f67b9dffcc08edc16ad3627.png)\n\n \n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and $Q(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+x{y}^{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-2\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,dx+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+x{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{y}^{2}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420413 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ebea952-0566-5c92-8d0e-e040e7712ee7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-{x}^{2}}\\,dy\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420407} {:answer/id #uuid "3f01f0a5-e9c8-5d76-9040-7592e7edc559" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}-1}^{1-{x}^{2}}2(x+y)\\,dy\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420408} {:answer/id #uuid "ad95bd1a-50e9-550a-940a-b1d62f28413d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-{x}^{2}}(x+y)\\,dy\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420409} {:answer/id #uuid "c5b67534-974d-530a-a5f5-af0136eb1850" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-{x}^{2}}^{0}\\,dy\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420410} {:answer/id #uuid "cfc4f4e1-426a-5f5a-951d-5f244fa030ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-{x}^{2}}^{{x}^{2}}\\,dy\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420411}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1ebea952-0566-5c92-8d0e-e040e7712ee7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-{x}^{2}}\\,dy\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420407} :answer-field/id #uuid "25ac563f-3cb9-5a93-a3a0-eb472a59f7c0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420412}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "426ff1c4-32bc-566d-87df-d5bec20e08eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-42074" :question/problem "Consider the closed curve $C,$ which is the boundary of the region enclosed by $y=1-{x}^{2}$ and $y=0.$ Express ${∮}_{C}({x}^{2}-y)\\,dx+(2x-{y}^{2})\\,dy$ as a repeated integral." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n \n\n![](images/a3/a3fe0bbc283a2eaacffad4b6be071f601781b6b77b2df0dd9b30abb22653c738.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={x}^{2}-y$ and $Q(x,y)=2x-{y}^{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-1,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 1,0\\le y\\le 1-{x}^{2}\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({x}^{2}-y)\\,dx+(2x-{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2-(-1))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Line Integral as a Repeated Integral Using Green's Theorem"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179744 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179606 :question/id #uuid "c1924df7-205f-512e-b024-72f5a654f062" :question/math-academy-id "e-9560" :question/problem "Calculate the flux of the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y) = (y - x)\\,\\mathbf{i} - (x + y )\\,\\mathbf{j}$ across the closed curve $C$ given by $\\mathbf r(t) = 4\\cos t\\,\\mathbf{i} + 4\\sin t\\,\\mathbf{j}$ for $t \\in [0,2\\pi).$" :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf{F} = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s = \\oint\\limits_{C}P\\,\\textrm d y - Q\\,\\textrm d x,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx = 4\\cos t, \\qquad\n\ty = 4\\sin t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =y-x=4(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t), \\\\ Q(x,y) & =-(x+y)=-4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(4(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t))\\cdot (4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-(-4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t))\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =16{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-{cos}^{2}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =16{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(-1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-16t{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =-16(2\\pi -0) \\\\  & =-32\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c9de11e-2195-5946-a19e-235667d39631" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048376 :topic/math-academy-id 3715 :topic/title "Flux in Two-Dimensional Vector Fields"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050666 :topic/math-academy-id 3705 :topic/title "Sums of Line Integrals With Respect to X and Y Over Parametric Curves"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180029 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ab07d163-0625-52d3-bb7a-5ead102e3c3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177844} {:answer/id #uuid "15190a02-e5af-5be2-8697-97e71684c08f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177845} {:answer/id #uuid "2605ce5a-8cc3-5767-bd42-b4df2fc57fe5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177846} {:answer/id #uuid "6f00d621-c436-5bd5-8625-07e82e475235" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177847} {:answer/id #uuid "6fdec857-84ed-52dd-8971-cb87df00b2a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177848}] :answer-field/id #uuid "d25cf8ea-2732-5072-883d-71745e726402" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177843}] :question/id #uuid "d6daea5f-9d1c-5bf1-b6af-12c3102e71d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-126717" :question/problem "Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x, y) = yi - xj$ across the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t) = 5\\cos ti + 5\\sin tj$ for $t ∈ [0, 2π)$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180040 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "942b4313-49e5-5dab-b44a-795f2625fcd0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177910} {:answer/id #uuid "c8e6a285-76dc-5b20-84a0-a2175ec4fdce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177911} {:answer/id #uuid "da3be3d9-2655-565a-b971-ebab43d181dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-18π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177912} {:answer/id #uuid "0cfab73e-541e-5dea-a884-264b4bd8ec97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177913} {:answer/id #uuid "1e469a0e-34c8-57f5-b73b-81f6838bd31b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177914}] :answer-field/id #uuid "dab28699-4fc7-5cb2-8f84-87f7666ba43c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177909}] :question/id #uuid "4edd3b9a-070a-593a-8f84-1b14657d2c07" :question/math-academy-id "q-127055" :question/problem "Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x, y) = (x + y)i + (y - x)j$ across the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t) = 3\\cos ti + 3\\sin tj$ for $t ∈ [0, 2π)$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262702 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1451c613-e020-5234-9207-ca8e2b426177" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260055} {:answer/id #uuid "545e7de3-77c0-5001-a9e8-6b1adc5f1466" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{16\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260589} {:answer/id #uuid "78acc197-697f-5085-b977-43333a81c4c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{32\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260932} {:answer/id #uuid "7b79330c-376e-54f4-b1b3-7e784422cfed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260951} {:answer/id #uuid "a87d06f4-05e6-5e25-a200-9da07f1daa38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{32\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261345}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1451c613-e020-5234-9207-ca8e2b426177" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260055} :answer-field/id #uuid "473e948e-e7b1-5fb0-bd43-b3e933074eaa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262248}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d6160f48-7946-52fa-bf89-f2b84f79ba1d" :question/math-academy-id "q-127060" :question/problem "Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=xy\\,i+{x}^{2}\\,j$ across the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =xy=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t, \\\\ Q(x,y) & ={x}^{2}=4{cos}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\text{d}u=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot (2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-(4{cos}^{2}⁡t)\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t+8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }16\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{1}-16{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262706 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2873f634-c695-583b-b477-95e3c1ffc07a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-60\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260232} {:answer/id #uuid "4babd7c8-f323-5126-a6c0-63a9f81a9e3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-30\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260524} {:answer/id #uuid "66277e5d-0782-5296-aac5-7543f0ac4723" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260762} {:answer/id #uuid "86598988-6527-5185-a16f-ee494f22fee7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261043} {:answer/id #uuid "a6bc2f7b-8b39-5063-8765-91cf86585fbd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261329}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a6bc2f7b-8b39-5063-8765-91cf86585fbd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261329} :answer-field/id #uuid "30577348-fd2b-554c-96e0-1728db3f4497" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262210}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "697f638b-1499-58a9-805f-6f4605d73510" :question/math-academy-id "q-127077" :question/problem "Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=x\\,i+y\\,j$ across the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =x=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot (6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-(6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot (-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =30{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =30{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }1\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =30(2\\pi -0) \\\\  & =60\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262711 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b2ab957-0579-5e99-8c2c-f53cec1a64ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260254} {:answer/id #uuid "515d246a-bf62-5921-88e9-813966854ecc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260564} {:answer/id #uuid "681d213e-13d8-58eb-9b2e-feeb4d760e83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260785} {:answer/id #uuid "787af263-8fcd-544e-a39d-804db798c96f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260930} {:answer/id #uuid "95210164-0d4e-5860-ac8f-9ab7e9038d97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261171}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "515d246a-bf62-5921-88e9-813966854ecc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260564} :answer-field/id #uuid "b00b1074-3f5c-50fa-8cd5-1bc5332b0ac4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262440}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "610929b6-feab-56c5-9108-52a1535e1a43" :question/math-academy-id "q-127081" :question/problem "Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=y\\,i+9{x}^{3}\\,j$ across the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =9{x}^{3}=9{cos}^{3}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral using the substitution $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=u,du=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,dt,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot (3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-(9{cos}^{3}⁡t)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =9{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{3}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-9{\\int }_{1}^{1}(u+{u}^{3})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262713 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6893a418-d322-5ab1-83ac-21e6380ffd8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-14\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260788} {:answer/id #uuid "8d1a6f11-e4dc-58f0-b3b9-20f5896018bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261107} {:answer/id #uuid "ad14b8d3-dc2a-51ae-93a4-3065094d2fff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261390} {:answer/id #uuid "c8457952-5981-52f2-8dd0-d726a811dc80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261647} {:answer/id #uuid "e9652b71-0ef3-57bb-b58d-c0c40977ff4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261917}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e9652b71-0ef3-57bb-b58d-c0c40977ff4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261917} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4fee625-b03f-59fc-b891-1aff5cd98622" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262451}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9148e698-2674-5a60-a839-9ed2c4895150" :question/math-academy-id "q-127139" :question/problem "Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)={x}^{2}\\,i+j$ across the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & ={x}^{2}=49{cos}^{2}⁡t, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral using the substitution $\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=u,du=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,dt,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(49{cos}^{2}⁡t)\\cdot (2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-(1)\\cdot (-7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }98{cos}^{3}⁡t+7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }98{cos}^{2}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }98(1-{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }98(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)+7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }98\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }-98{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }98\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{0}^{0}-98{u}^{2}\\,du \\\\  & =(98\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t){|}_{0}^{2\\pi }+0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262714 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8337cc5b-cbe0-5afa-9274-103e3f498697" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261015} {:answer/id #uuid "8d8c2924-c012-51ca-aa9f-ebee29edb99a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261114} {:answer/id #uuid "9958d4dc-9c0d-556d-9236-c69dc7ace6dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "192\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261204} {:answer/id #uuid "9c54fcc6-cb8b-5805-9834-9c56673a0e9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261228} {:answer/id #uuid "b08ecf9c-f351-5fc2-9328-9e808bafe62f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "384\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261434}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b08ecf9c-f351-5fc2-9328-9e808bafe62f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "384\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261434} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2ba8e33-8924-5bcc-b848-378e5d728fa7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262473}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fc62686d-15ed-5e7b-a83e-66fbf5f6c46f" :question/math-academy-id "q-127140" :question/problem "Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=4x\\,i+4y\\,j$ across the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =4x=4(8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=32\\operatorname{cos}⁡t, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =4y=4(6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=24\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(32\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot (6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-(24\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot (-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }192({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }192\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =192t{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =192(2\\pi -0) \\\\  & =384\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421762 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "093b5f1d-7084-5cca-af48-56a7c276ea5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421730} {:answer/id #uuid "1755e313-29bb-5235-930c-5480eea8e580" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{25e}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421732} {:answer/id #uuid "2a42ddb5-8533-50f3-9230-498c83bae1a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7e}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421735} {:answer/id #uuid "93070110-72d1-593c-aaa7-f43291b04560" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7e}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421745} {:answer/id #uuid "c7b8c70b-0bf4-5466-85d5-56e8bfa6fc37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25e}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421749}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "093b5f1d-7084-5cca-af48-56a7c276ea5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421730} :answer-field/id #uuid "452bd17e-660a-568a-8108-39d6d0770162" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421756}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "90df2f56-7b61-5a49-aa6d-4d0c58b35b14" :question/math-academy-id "q-127062" :question/problem "Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)={e}^{y}\\,i+{e}^{x}\\,j$ across the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & ={e}^{y}={e}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}, \\\\ Q(x,y) & ={e}^{x}={e}^{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral using the substitutions $u=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,du=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,dt$ and $v=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,dv=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,dt,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }({e}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t})\\cdot (4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-({e}^{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t})\\cdot (-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }({e}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t})\\cdot (4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t-{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }({e}^{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t})\\cdot (-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{0}{e}^{u}\\,du-{\\int }_{1}^{1}{e}^{v}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421763 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29d3016e-ea1f-57be-ad3f-89bd52e2b595" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-15\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421733} {:answer/id #uuid "29d80bea-8d5f-5d6e-9d57-a11322c402b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-30\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421734} {:answer/id #uuid "2cd76549-ed5c-561a-a07f-a7506fbab6d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421736} {:answer/id #uuid "3cf1e009-304c-5363-9a50-24170560d53d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421738} {:answer/id #uuid "eb22ea92-b3d9-59e8-9fe5-49b32e301c4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421753}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3cf1e009-304c-5363-9a50-24170560d53d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421738} :answer-field/id #uuid "8411ac91-aa07-508a-9904-aa0a020877bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421758}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b2007897-e589-5e81-a8ff-d3a65c150dee" :question/math-academy-id "q-127082" :question/problem "Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=x\\,i+(x+y)\\,j$ across the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =x+y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral using the substitution $\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=u,du=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,dt,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot (5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot (-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }15{cos}^{2}⁡t+9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+15{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }15\\,\\text{d}t+9{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }15\\,\\text{d}t+9{\\int }_{0}^{0}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =15t{|}_{0}^{2\\pi }+0 \\\\  & =15(2\\pi -0) \\\\  & =30\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Flux of a Vector Field Over a Closed Curve in Parametric Form"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179745 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179607 :question/id #uuid "e9d8e6c2-d346-52fb-918a-5d5c95cd986c" :question/math-academy-id "e-9561" :question/problem "Calculate the flux of the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y) = xy\\,\\mathbf{i} + x^2\\,\\mathbf{j}$ across the ellipse $C$ with equation $9x^2 + y^2 = 9.$" :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf{F} = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s = \\oint\\limits_{C}P\\,\\textrm d y - Q\\,\\textrm d x,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nFirst, we write the equation of our curve as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{1^2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{3^2} = 1.\n$$\n\nThe closed curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal and vertical semiaxes $1$ and $3,$ respectively. It can be parameterized in the counterclockwise direction as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\cos{t}\\,\\mathbf{i} +3\\sin{t}\\,\\mathbf{j},\n\\qquad 0 \\leq t \\leq 2\\pi.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx = \\cos t, \\qquad\ny = 3\\sin t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =xy=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t, \\\\ Q(x,y) & ={x}^{2}={cos}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral using the substitution $u=\\cos t, \\textrm d u = -\\sin t\\,\\textrm d t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot (3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-({cos}^{2}⁡t)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }10\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{1}^{1}10{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ea50c3f-d401-54c5-868c-b67992bf18ee" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048540 :topic/math-academy-id 3703 :topic/title "Sums of Line Integrals With Respect to X and Y"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180030 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1856b101-7baf-59fd-834b-982658a922fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-25\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260088} {:answer/id #uuid "1f19942f-78b6-5bf3-b50c-884e00feb139" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260143} {:answer/id #uuid "4a3a8518-8872-5062-8257-1de19938dc58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260514} {:answer/id #uuid "a5164cfa-c311-554e-b1f3-9bea6aceb7da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261310} {:answer/id #uuid "d560e271-d185-5a8f-b214-1548caab5e90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261753}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a5164cfa-c311-554e-b1f3-9bea6aceb7da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261310} :answer-field/id #uuid "f60a314a-1658-5f43-adea-650d5ae0b480" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262551}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f1687af9-fdc5-568f-9b8e-74b394a32f6f" :question/math-academy-id "q-126719" :question/problem "Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=x\\,i+5\\,j$ across the ellipse $C$ with equation $25{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=25.$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nFirst, we write the equation of our curve as\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{1}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{5}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nThe closed curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal and vertical semiaxes $1$ and $5,$ respectively. It can be parameterized in the counterclockwise direction as\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral using the double-angle formula for cosine, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot (5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-(5)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }5{cos}^{2}⁡t+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =5{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+1}{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={5(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)}{4}+\\frac{1}{2}t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =5(0+\\pi -1)-5(0-1) \\\\  & =5\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180041 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bfd04950-8c76-5e5c-bd3b-57f33b66e8e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{10}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177916} {:answer/id #uuid "3d33d012-f495-55e4-a694-7649d9356119" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177917} {:answer/id #uuid "470219b9-52ad-5f5d-85b1-f6487d2469f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{26}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177918} {:answer/id #uuid "4f3d558e-c45c-5b6d-ba67-dbe452d8b64f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177919} {:answer/id #uuid "77a8d59a-d4b3-536c-9bf3-7c9b158878e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{26}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177920}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4f3d558e-c45c-5b6d-ba67-dbe452d8b64f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177919} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f6d5bae-0477-56a7-a550-3209e91d41b3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177915}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "14039085-c7d5-567d-835b-0c3eaccf0c38" :question/math-academy-id "q-127141" :question/problem "Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)={y}^{2}\\,i+xy\\,j$ across the ellipse $C$ with equation $4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}=36.$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nFirst, we write the equation of our curve as\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{3}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nThe closed curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal and vertical semiaxes $3$ and $2,$ respectively. It can be parameterized in the counterclockwise direction as\n\n$$\nr(t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & ={y}^{2}=4{sin}^{2}⁡t, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =xy=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(4{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\cdot (2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-(6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot (-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }8{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+18{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }26{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{1}26{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262708 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "002f13da-1ead-583a-ac5e-651659fba636" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-24\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259873} {:answer/id #uuid "65205214-2e2f-5e83-866b-28dbde2e8142" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-48\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260748} {:answer/id #uuid "658cf6f1-e7cb-54a1-be31-60a0e04e3691" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260755} {:answer/id #uuid "ff2c884f-9604-537a-a609-b6534930561a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262112} {:answer/id #uuid "ff7715db-cabc-54c2-8f21-10a71cfd6580" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262114}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff2c884f-9604-537a-a609-b6534930561a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262112} :answer-field/id #uuid "2179ad4a-3652-5bdd-bc61-3c3fe5e735a6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262181}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "16f3ca45-80c3-5384-9b7f-eb8d6edbed52" :question/math-academy-id "q-127079" :question/problem "Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=3x\\,i+3y\\,j$ across the ellipse $C$ with equation $4{x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}=64.$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nFirst, we write the equation of our curve as\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{4}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nThe closed curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal and vertical semiaxes $4$ and $2,$ respectively. It can be parameterized in the counterclockwise direction as\n\n$$\nr(t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =3x=12\\operatorname{cos}⁡t, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =3y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(12\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot (2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-(6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }24{cos}^{2}⁡t+24{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }24\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =24t\\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =24(2\\pi -0) \\\\  & =48\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262710 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06286b15-8da9-5506-8604-f3bd7afc10d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259929} {:answer/id #uuid "1d1a02d9-d7f1-5166-bd4e-5530469e75d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260122} {:answer/id #uuid "33138d4c-75fd-51c7-ada1-ca9a1c3bda26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260337} {:answer/id #uuid "5445d32b-9ec9-5c1c-9e46-7663e482e5cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260587} {:answer/id #uuid "abde1db8-a335-505b-b987-fbee5cecdacd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261376}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "33138d4c-75fd-51c7-ada1-ca9a1c3bda26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260337} :answer-field/id #uuid "20e946c1-64ee-5863-81ee-bad217dfe5fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262179}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9c13e73f-8b16-5eac-90ca-cc2a4f61af68" :question/math-academy-id "q-127080" :question/problem "Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=(x-16y)\\,i+(x+y)\\,j$ across the ellipse $C$ with equation ${x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}=16.$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nFirst, we write the equation of our curve as\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{4}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{1}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nThe closed curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal and vertical semiaxes $4$ and $1,$ respectively. It can be parameterized in the counterclockwise direction as\n\n$$\nr(t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =x-16y=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-16\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =x+y=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-16\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }4{cos}^{2}⁡t-16\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+16\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+4{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }4\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4t\\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =4(2\\pi -0) \\\\  & =8\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262715 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e1109ed-b141-519b-a9c0-7e14a71dad93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "54" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260290} {:answer/id #uuid "9c868b9a-1bd9-5569-9736-5eeff1693bf8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261230} {:answer/id #uuid "d2368728-193a-54c0-a8d0-19ec801f6e17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261726} {:answer/id #uuid "d923b6e6-b8d4-5b4d-b3b3-1e5f1ccd3861" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261792} {:answer/id #uuid "ff0167ac-6b4e-5d5c-aee1-3a42390d42e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262111}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9c868b9a-1bd9-5569-9736-5eeff1693bf8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261230} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e7bee21-7d21-5f74-b594-30d563e58eac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262334}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "22f1827e-9056-5ca5-89c4-8b8f59040ce8" :question/math-academy-id "q-127142" :question/problem "Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=7\\,i+2\\,j$ across the circle $C$ with equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=36.$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nThe closed curve $C$ is a circle centered at the origin with the radius $6.$ It can be parameterized in the counterclockwise direction as\n\n$$\nr(t)=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =7, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(7)\\cdot (6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-(2)\\cdot (-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }42\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+12\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(42\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-12\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =(0-12)-(0-12) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262716 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a1393ce9-919a-5a3f-a8c5-3ce471d06ed6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261276} {:answer/id #uuid "a40cd9e0-094a-53e2-bea7-72696c9a5f5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261304} {:answer/id #uuid "a5d88754-afb8-5215-960d-6c87c992d632" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261315} {:answer/id #uuid "c6e3c02b-04d6-59c7-b162-6bd0c1725f86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261634} {:answer/id #uuid "c73cd479-dfcc-5a7b-abdd-2aab64decf0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261638}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c73cd479-dfcc-5a7b-abdd-2aab64decf0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261638} :answer-field/id #uuid "f19e243a-d229-55b5-b933-2f21b1dc4b86" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262543}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "79c34a7b-b623-515b-8476-bf469364ea95" :question/math-academy-id "q-127143" :question/problem "Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)={y}^{3}\\,i+{x}^{3}\\,j$ across the circle $C$ with equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nFirst, we write the equation of our curve as\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={1}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe closed curve $C$ is a circle centered at the origin with the radius $1.$ It can be parameterized in the counterclockwise direction as\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & ={y}^{3}={sin}^{3}⁡t, \\\\ Q(x,y) & ={x}^{3}={cos}^{3}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }({sin}^{3}⁡t)\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-({cos}^{3}⁡t)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{sin}^{3}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{3}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t({sin}^{2}+{cos}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{0}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262717 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e7e8e10-abcb-56ed-beb0-0cb36f4ca76e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-18\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260425} {:answer/id #uuid "b834cd68-c467-5c15-8f91-57a9cd6ebdf6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261490} {:answer/id #uuid "c105bc88-2067-5448-8851-b4aed4ed98a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261579} {:answer/id #uuid "ec49c88b-42ae-5fd2-ac78-38df2fb9d69d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261949} {:answer/id #uuid "fad96982-5248-5746-9c49-6b8b91690477" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262069}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3e7e8e10-abcb-56ed-beb0-0cb36f4ca76e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-18\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260425} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d9b077c-868b-5fb4-af2a-a019d69ad08b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262404}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f3f2013e-6c87-5135-8bb8-8a0a551136de" :question/math-academy-id "q-127144" :question/problem "Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=(y-x)\\,i-(x+y)\\,j$ across the circle $C$ with equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nFirst, we write the equation of our curve as\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={3}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe closed curve $C$ is a circle centered at the origin with the radius $1.$ It can be parameterized in the counterclockwise direction as\n\n$$\nr(t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =y-x=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =-(x+y)=-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot (3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-(-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot (-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =9{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-{cos}^{2}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =9{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }-({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-9{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-9t\\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =-9(2\\pi -0) \\\\  & =-18\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262718 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b7ec63b-9d52-510c-b90f-d508cdf66df0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259982} {:answer/id #uuid "205ea45c-44f8-56c3-ad7d-0bf1b3454377" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260153} {:answer/id #uuid "417601b4-9041-518b-b1fe-5f87ca522ec4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260447} {:answer/id #uuid "585a5637-623b-5976-b2c1-8831d3e1f208" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260629} {:answer/id #uuid "eda13820-313c-5809-816f-d91f3326152f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261965}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "417601b4-9041-518b-b1fe-5f87ca522ec4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260447} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd0f258d-a686-56be-9950-ae986e7e5ad1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262567}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bf94cd2c-9106-5cc5-9f8f-bee0a724f45f" :question/math-academy-id "q-127145" :question/problem "Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=y\\,i-2x\\,j$ across the circle $C$ with equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16.$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nFirst, we write the equation of our curve as\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={4}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe closed curve $C$ is a circle centered at the origin with the radius $4.$ It can be parameterized in the counterclockwise direction as\n\n$$\nr(t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =-2x=-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot (4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-(-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(-16\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-16{\\int }_{0}^{0}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262719 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3cb7c321-8c66-5fb7-96c2-0437ee47dcbf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260415} {:answer/id #uuid "5fea40a6-440c-5c51-92de-dfd95b183492" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260704} {:answer/id #uuid "dbb0d441-3294-517d-a38a-f662036aa525" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261818} {:answer/id #uuid "ead25eb6-815a-5d8e-824e-3e16a08a1082" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-24\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261935} {:answer/id #uuid "f6dcfd74-7f22-5bdc-9f18-a7450433e36c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262033}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ead25eb6-815a-5d8e-824e-3e16a08a1082" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-24\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261935} :answer-field/id #uuid "e257f11d-b166-5f39-948f-09f39d7ae5f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262522}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "07fc4782-ce17-55e6-b2c6-9fdd11a42eea" :question/math-academy-id "q-127146" :question/problem "Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=(1-x)\\,i+(x-y)\\,j$ across the ellipse $C$ with equation $16{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}=144.$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nFirst, we write the equation of our curve as\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{3}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{4}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nThe closed curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal and vertical semiaxes $3$ and $4,$ respectively. It can be parameterized in the counterclockwise direction as\n\n$$\nr(t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =1-x=1-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =x-y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(1-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot (4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot (-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-12{cos}^{2}⁡t+9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-12{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t+9{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t-12{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t+9{\\int }_{0}^{0}u\\,\\text{d}u-12{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{|}_{0}^{2\\pi }+0-12t\\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =4(0-0)-12(2\\pi -0) \\\\  & =-24\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262720 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e374ae4-5835-5934-a507-a409fd4323a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260538} {:answer/id #uuid "5614e116-ef4d-5042-8e46-b643dd68cc4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260612} {:answer/id #uuid "7b0140fc-b923-5813-a89f-c3608038ef00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260948} {:answer/id #uuid "b7425b11-60ac-5890-ba20-5dbb9f67e966" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261485} {:answer/id #uuid "c6b3ea07-0a25-5176-81f6-6d1cdf6d1277" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261628}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5614e116-ef4d-5042-8e46-b643dd68cc4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260612} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f921ac0-a8e9-5b23-849a-a963b93c6d53" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262151}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4b077b66-1e99-5133-b08d-623645791b05" :question/math-academy-id "q-127148" :question/problem "Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=8\\,i-(x+3)\\,j$ across the circle $C$ with equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nFirst, we write the equation of our curve as\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={2}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe closed curve $C$ is a circle centered at the origin with the radius $2.$ It can be parameterized in the counterclockwise direction as\n\n$$\nr(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =8, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =x+3=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral using the double-angle formula for sine, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(8)\\cdot (2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-(-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-3)\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }16\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }16\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(16\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262721 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6d6e8dc0-de0b-5246-baf1-2e88a94ead89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260835} {:answer/id #uuid "86aa0ca4-2fef-5ea7-ab52-7326ec1150e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-75\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261048} {:answer/id #uuid "945c2bc9-3eea-5671-ac58-7ea3e0a8ed17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "150\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261168} {:answer/id #uuid "b7033798-e10d-5128-ab9b-7bc561366c39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-100\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261481} {:answer/id #uuid "dcaaf559-e7cf-5424-90bb-d31bc261f4d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "50\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261824}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6d6e8dc0-de0b-5246-baf1-2e88a94ead89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260835} :answer-field/id #uuid "23816b2f-7629-5e86-9bf6-14c8e6ebed0d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262186}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5660b13f-68bb-56f7-8d7c-83ccf48f3c64" :question/math-academy-id "q-127149" :question/problem "Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=x\\,i-5\\,j$ across the circle $C$ with equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=25.$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nFirst, we write the equation of our curve as\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={5}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe closed curve $C$ is a circle centered at the origin with the radius $5.$ It can be parameterized in the counterclockwise direction as\n\n$$\nr(t)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =x=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral using the double-angle formula for cosine, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot (5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-(-5)\\cdot (-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }25{cos}^{2}⁡t-25\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =25{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+1}{2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =25(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)}{4}+\\frac{1}{2}t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =25(0+\\pi +1)-(0+1) \\\\  & =25\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421760 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37855248-eec6-54d6-abfc-d67f62c8a619" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{e}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421737} {:answer/id #uuid "77c54161-201d-5420-8d49-397874bb229d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{2}(1-{e}^{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421743} {:answer/id #uuid "9a40d272-dad6-5203-a06f-4c1b6242a085" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}(1-{e}^{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421746} {:answer/id #uuid "cb802d80-52ae-51ef-9629-c1c85410bffa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421750} {:answer/id #uuid "fbbebcc7-a768-5300-b557-75982c0cd1f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421754}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cb802d80-52ae-51ef-9629-c1c85410bffa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421750} :answer-field/id #uuid "b62610d8-6a5e-55a5-8cf3-f983d7201b32" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421759}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3e7323f9-19dc-5d6f-a8c7-1664ab5ee0f5" :question/math-academy-id "q-127147" :question/problem "Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)={e}^{y}\\,i+{e}^{x}\\,j$ across the ellipse $C$ with equation $4{x}^{2}+25{y}^{2}=100.$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nFirst, we write the equation of our curve as\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{5}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nThe closed curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal and vertical semiaxes $5$ and $2,$ respectively. It can be parameterized in the counterclockwise direction as\n\n$$\nr(t)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & ={e}^{y}={e}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}, \\\\ Q(x,y) & ={e}^{x}={e}^{5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral using the substitutions $u=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,du=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,dt$ and $v=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,dv=-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,dt,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }({e}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t})\\cdot (2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-({e}^{5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t})\\cdot (-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }({e}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t})\\cdot (2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t-{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }({e}^{5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t})\\cdot (-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{0}{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u+{\\int }_{1}^{1}{e}^{v}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Flux of a Vector Field Over a Closed Curve in Cartesian Form"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179746 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179608 :question/id #uuid "337d9d7e-5488-5e6d-af34-e01d22d3d0b9" :question/math-academy-id "e-9574" :question/problem "![](images/6e/6eebbc5ae9f548f5b00de9a12d454fa788884bf73ad46a1065eab0d26330a921.png)\n\nThe curve $C$ is given by $y = \\dfrac14x^2$ from the point $\\left(0,0\\right)$ to the point $\\left(4,4\\right).$ Calculate the flux of the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y) = -2\\,\\mathbf{i} + x\\,\\mathbf{j}$ through $C,$ measured with respect to the unit normal vector $\\mathbf n$ on $C,$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf{F} = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ measured with respect to a unit normal vector $\\mathbf n$ on $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s = \\int\\limits_{C}P\\,\\textrm d y - Q\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nTo apply this formula, \n\n- the curve should be parameterized as $\\mathbf r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and\n\n- the unit normal vector $\\mathbf n$ *must* be a $90^\\circ$ clockwise rotation of the unit tangent vector $\\mathbf T(t).$\n\nParameterizing the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx = t, \\qquad\ny = \\dfrac{t^2}{4},\n\\qquad\nt\\in \\left[0,4\\right].\n$$\n\n \n\nThis parameterization traverses the curve from the point $(0,0)$ to the point $(4,4).$ Therefore, $\\mathbf n$ is indeed a $90^\\circ$ clockwise rotation of $\\mathbf T,$ so we can apply the above formula for the flux. \n\n![](images/2b/2b94e24344f600fc02ec5ecac6c1e0ee4c6a5450132753a0a5ac5fceaa9aa1fc.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =-2, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =x=t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)=1, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{t}^{2}}{4})=\\frac{t}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}(-2)\\cdot (\\frac{t}{2})-(t)\\cdot (1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}-2t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{t}^{2}{|}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =-16-0 \\\\  & =-16\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "83a9ea5a-dcc8-51ed-90bc-dd684ef71ca9" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180038 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "153220d6-7689-5056-8b5b-6d10695accb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260060} {:answer/id #uuid "39ef2d5d-86ef-5bc0-a9ec-82673d7b7699" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260393} {:answer/id #uuid "7523559d-4e43-59f2-bd01-34c48fcecddf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260899} {:answer/id #uuid "9f78043b-d21c-5406-8dbe-ff8fab366365" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261259} {:answer/id #uuid "cfe74df2-842d-5ac2-840e-8b0249def4b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261711}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "153220d6-7689-5056-8b5b-6d10695accb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260060} :answer-field/id #uuid "e164db38-546a-5334-8c07-335889e10d58" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262518}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e0b1aae8-7058-5a37-a641-ca7e8583e806" :question/math-academy-id "q-126940" :question/problem "![](images/c7/c71395cfe5877546509f24656ba23f0883cbd3ee8c145c10abae9600c8bd5c4a.png)\n\nCalculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=x\\,i+y\\,j$ through the curve $C$ given by $r(t)=t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,1],$ measured with respect to the unit normal vector $n$ on $C,$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ measured with respect to a unit normal vector $n$ on $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo apply this formula, \n\n- the curve should be parameterized as $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and\n\n- the unit normal vector $n$ *must* be a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of the unit tangent vector $T(t).$\n\nIn this case, $n$ is indeed a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of $T,$ so we can apply the above formula. \n\n![](images/e9/e90c36e34c6ab51f15707685a22c8bd2c749b9857ca24c59631f09dfbda495c6.png)\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=t,\\,y={t}^{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =x=t, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =y={t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)=1, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})=2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(t)\\cdot (2t)-({t}^{2})\\cdot (1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180039 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6b1e968e-8930-58be-ab71-3bd0f25e502c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260814} {:answer/id #uuid "8333823b-23fc-55f7-8631-0c3b55eaeefe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261014} {:answer/id #uuid "a9d2cd70-36ee-5747-b7fa-0641fc3c4792" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261359} {:answer/id #uuid "cc8eec96-250f-548c-8bb5-2b5e1f480ed0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261676} {:answer/id #uuid "fb24cd87-3b97-54a1-84bc-1c02515203f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262071}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cc8eec96-250f-548c-8bb5-2b5e1f480ed0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261676} :answer-field/id #uuid "a2711ee6-8fbc-5466-bb32-b1817aaad6f4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262415}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "72bfedb9-24a7-5361-9689-9e032cb56805" :question/math-academy-id "q-126943" :question/problem "![](images/27/27fe7019b715ca8f4d07a3f5e7f6ddc312288a135cb1dcb197aacbb98765cdf9.png)\n\nThe curve $C$ is given by ${y}^{2}=4x$ from the point $(0,0)$ to the point $(1,2).$ Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=2\\,i-4\\,j$ through $C,$ measured with respect to the unit normal vector $n$ on $C,$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ measured with respect to a unit normal vector $n$ on $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo apply this formula, \n\n- the curve should be parameterized as $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and\n\n- the unit normal vector $n$ *must* be a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of the unit tangent vector $T(t).$\n\nParameterizing the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2},\\,y=t,\\,t\\in [0,2].\n$$\n\n \n\nThis parameterization traverses the curve from the point $(0,0)$ to the point $(1,2.)$ Therefore, $n$ is indeed a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of $T,$ so we can apply the above formula for the flux. \n\n![](images/b7/b7ac1ce4a79528f5660b8f898739da72489ebe8e8fc156020efae77a103fbec4.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =2, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2})=\\frac{1}{2}t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(2)\\cdot (1)-(-4)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}2+2t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(2t+{t}^{2}){|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =(2\\cdot 2+{2}^{2})-0 \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262722 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "010cedc7-04ff-5f16-9a1f-085d388313fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259881} {:answer/id #uuid "09626ec8-e17c-5b36-b09e-5054afa4cc2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259961} {:answer/id #uuid "1a4cb0f9-d69d-5c81-a93c-3faf3d8337ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260103} {:answer/id #uuid "944bcd06-41b3-556d-a4d8-484681371ab7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261167} {:answer/id #uuid "d83ebeec-fe9c-5425-9a08-bb91b4e72a73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261781}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1a4cb0f9-d69d-5c81-a93c-3faf3d8337ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260103} :answer-field/id #uuid "901c84ca-e440-500b-974e-5ca5b232abfb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262378}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "42e5f742-bcfa-5cc0-b6a7-83f64e545deb" :question/math-academy-id "q-127151" :question/problem "![](images/0e/0ea0204d3e1f230efcde4b82da36b1986af732cdf2a01ad868aa0f6bf7414326.png)\n\nCalculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=4y\\,i+(2x-1)\\,j$ through the curve $C$ given by $r(t)={t}^{2}\\,i+t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,1],$ measured with respect to the unit normal vector $n$ on $C,$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ measured with respect to a unit normal vector $n$ on $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo apply this formula, \n\n- the curve should be parameterized as $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and\n\n- the unit normal vector $n$ *must* be a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of the unit tangent vector $T(t).$\n\nIn this case, $n$ is indeed a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of $T,$ so we can apply the above formula. \n\n![](images/59/59e503e0c4d950f030891bbaeb7ed890426ef470a1e1dae89d27eabb8dd591a6.png)\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx={t}^{2},\\,y=t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =4y=4t, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =2x-1=2{t}^{2}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})=2t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(4t)\\cdot (1)-(2{t}^{2}-1)\\cdot (2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}4t-4{t}^{3}+2t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}6t-4{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3{t}^{2}-{t}^{4}{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(3-1)-(0-0){|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262723 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6358a3a8-f845-54cc-badf-92a331e8c75f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260729} {:answer/id #uuid "93ddeddc-85f8-5387-a587-854777edee72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261160} {:answer/id #uuid "b17cc72a-30d4-5aec-8517-fea806ee64e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261442} {:answer/id #uuid "e5d5adf3-22c1-5a80-9054-3b1037ee3f8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261894} {:answer/id #uuid "f71e0832-1bd3-5aaf-8f23-39e7e2f6b562" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262036}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f71e0832-1bd3-5aaf-8f23-39e7e2f6b562" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262036} :answer-field/id #uuid "bad31031-4ff6-504d-befe-9c279e836730" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262464}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "22f56e61-1496-5d08-b396-c18e68744d11" :question/math-academy-id "q-127153" :question/problem "![](images/fb/fbfe241043e9014c1b633d163cf7e06234db70702a9052297b5df8b9df3fd82f.png)\n\nCalculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=x\\,i+{y}^{2}\\,j$ through the curve $C$ given by $r(t)=\\frac{1}{t}\\,i+t\\,j$ for $t\\in [\\frac{1}{2},2],$ measured with respect to the unit normal vector $n$ on $C,$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ measured with respect to a unit normal vector $n$ on $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo apply this formula, \n\n- the curve should be parameterized as $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and\n\n- the unit normal vector $n$ *must* be a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of the unit tangent vector $T(t).$\n\nIn this case, $n$ is indeed a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of $T,$ so we can apply the above formula. \n\n![](images/d4/d432857e3350dfcb70a2a00b66ad9b86bc207382edd277c2169be0834c1710e0.png)\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{t},\\,y=t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =x=\\frac{1}{t}, \\\\ Q(x,y) & ={y}^{2}={t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{t})=-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{2}(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{2}(\\frac{1}{t})\\cdot (1)-({t}^{2})\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{2}\\frac{1}{t}+1\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+t){|}_{1/2}^{2} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+2)-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262734 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2fd158a1-8e1c-5409-9f7d-cc713afefd91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260308} {:answer/id #uuid "49e0677d-703c-5fd2-bba6-5d10e8428357" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260512} {:answer/id #uuid "afbd580d-7551-55b1-a2ab-a54c76c17535" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261427} {:answer/id #uuid "d49ca082-e6c8-5be2-9a1d-8f3250a52777" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261749} {:answer/id #uuid "f489697f-1e0a-5833-b73d-49cd2c52c047" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262011}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "49e0677d-703c-5fd2-bba6-5d10e8428357" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260512} :answer-field/id #uuid "fec87113-a1f7-5b61-8ed5-83eb792efa1a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262568}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d9d8ed81-5fd6-581e-8bd7-3dd3517c5bfe" :question/math-academy-id "q-127223" :question/problem "![](images/41/414611a90c2227ff163d40a4f6f560bc67c471fa0f35724e459cec280e4904f9.png)\n\nCalculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=4\\,i+2\\,j$ through the curve $C$ given by $r(t)=\\frac{t}{2}\\,i+\\frac{1}{2t}\\,j$ for $t\\in [\\frac{1}{2},2],$ measured with respect to the unit normal vector $n$ on $C,$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ measured with respect to a unit normal vector $n$ on $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo apply this formula, \n\n- the curve should be parameterized as $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and\n\n- the unit normal vector $n$ *must* be a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of the unit tangent vector $T(t).$\n\nIn this case, $n$ is indeed a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of $T,$ so we can apply the above formula. \n\n![](images/11/11376e6ef18b151a6dd1a02dd09f20b72bce3160c718f4095b4f08b23721ff1f.png)\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{t}{2},\\,y=\\frac{1}{2t}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =4, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{t}{2})=\\frac{1}{2}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{2t})=-\\frac{1}{2{t}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{2}(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{2}(4)\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2{t}^{2}})-(2)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{2}-\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}}-1\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(\\frac{2}{t}-t){|}_{1/2}^{2} \\\\  & =(1-2)-(4-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262735 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ef40240-5dea-5969-9bf5-aa4953cf23a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260016} {:answer/id #uuid "57f7d7af-cfe4-5280-876a-0df898cd965b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260625} {:answer/id #uuid "6e49d8ea-e28e-5c91-87e1-566eb88277b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260846} {:answer/id #uuid "da6bceb7-3212-5a3d-8999-7aab1e79ca62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261804} {:answer/id #uuid "f232b69c-3511-56ed-b069-32c42592bb1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261993}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "57f7d7af-cfe4-5280-876a-0df898cd965b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260625} :answer-field/id #uuid "01d0cb2f-2881-58f1-a7e4-d56c754b767d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262128}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1caebf8b-fbe2-5515-a0df-5bb50a3ced16" :question/math-academy-id "q-127227" :question/problem "![](images/85/85b4da8c0fa35867871b6254ba20f505b7596f2c97226adb2ed01339fe4cbedf.png)\n\nCalculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=5{e}^{-x}\\,i+4\\,j$ through the curve $C$ given by $r(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\,i+t\\,j$ for $t\\in [1,e],$ measured with respect to the unit normal vector $n$ on $C,$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ measured with respect to a unit normal vector $n$ on $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo apply this formula, \n\n- the curve should be parameterized as $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and\n\n- the unit normal vector $n$ *must* be a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of the unit tangent vector $T(t).$\n\nIn this case, $n$ is indeed a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of $T,$ so we can apply the above formula. \n\n![](images/55/559bbc20aa7d47eaad47a8975f80c8efb91bd03bc816357bc6047675dc755c9b.png)\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,\\,y=t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =5{e}^{-x}=5{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}=\\frac{5}{t}, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)=\\frac{1}{t}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}(\\frac{5}{t})\\cdot (1)-(4)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{t})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{1}{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡t{|}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262738 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b4f1bb5-5af8-54dc-b770-fd3f05a8066c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260522} {:answer/id #uuid "5d592e34-b162-5fde-8afc-943efe3e8ed5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260678} {:answer/id #uuid "ad49cef3-b98d-5033-a436-5ffab9049391" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261392} {:answer/id #uuid "aeb8232e-688e-5fce-98f8-d1fc7d49d436" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261415} {:answer/id #uuid "fdb10982-6a16-5384-8d48-742898dfa1f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262100}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b4f1bb5-5af8-54dc-b770-fd3f05a8066c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260522} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c9c8acf-0dab-51c5-ade8-bb82d29c0b7f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262202}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d7df129a-7685-5a03-8c7b-340092011e9b" :question/math-academy-id "q-127233" :question/problem "![](images/9b/9bb15934c271a17851342b9032f5096f48e7ac9ee4e149af92ac1010dea46d88.png)\n\nCalculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=3xy\\,i+(2x+1)\\,j$ through the curve $C$ given by $r(t)=t\\,i+t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,1],$ measured with respect to the unit normal vector $n$ on $C,$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ measured with respect to a unit normal vector $n$ on $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo apply this formula, \n\n- the curve should be parameterized as $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and\n\n- the unit normal vector $n$ *must* be a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of the unit tangent vector $T(t).$\n\nIn this case, $n$ is indeed a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of $T,$ so we can apply the above formula. \n\n![](images/0e/0e751adb9f44d24ba89af318fe5f063b1be146bbae45d05fc2dd9a99bef259f2.png)\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=t,\\,y=t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =3xy=3{t}^{2}, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =2x+1=2t+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)=1, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(3{t}^{2})(1)-(2t+1)(1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}3{t}^{2}-2t-1\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =({t}^{3}-{t}^{2}-t){|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(1-1-1)-(0-0-0) \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262739 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07034c2e-8af8-57f8-a980-3075761e8455" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259942} {:answer/id #uuid "137e64d0-7b45-5b0d-b600-d6bc9ea46c28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{14}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260047} {:answer/id #uuid "639ba164-e39e-53eb-a66d-858b18447c60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260735} {:answer/id #uuid "99c2bd41-610f-5d52-9a7d-8d782bd6205a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261206} {:answer/id #uuid "d74d9236-a4bb-589b-961a-53f185bb7d01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261768}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "137e64d0-7b45-5b0d-b600-d6bc9ea46c28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{14}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260047} :answer-field/id #uuid "275aecf1-178a-5df7-a9f4-4a5476f8a4ac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262194}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b74be94c-a6f2-5d0a-8660-699d2ff2d45b" :question/math-academy-id "q-127244" :question/problem "![](images/60/6043a1f88474e2c282938d3b3f0193002b6e11b5fb6d16292fea43d63292d1b0.png)\n\nThe curve $C$ is given by $y={x}^{3}$ from the point $(0,0)$ to the point $(1,1).$ Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=-3{x}^{2}\\,i+\\,j$ through $C,$ measured with respect to the unit normal vector $n$ on $C,$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ measured with respect to a unit normal vector $n$ on $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo apply this formula, \n\n- the curve should be parameterized as $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and\n\n- the unit normal vector $n$ *must* be a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of the unit tangent vector $T(t).$\n\nParameterizing the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=t,\\,y={t}^{3},\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\n \n\nThis parameterization traverses the curve from the point $(0,0)$ to the point $(1,1).$ Therefore, $n$ is indeed a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of $T,$ so we can apply the above formula for the flux. \n\n![](images/1d/1d54fe3c07f04a7d05567942e3eea6efaf1cb516070a5d7cc31ef726c0ddb423.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =-3{x}^{2}=-3{t}^{2}, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)=1, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3})=3{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-3{t}^{2})\\cdot (3{t}^{2})-(1)\\cdot (1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}-9{t}^{4}-1\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(-\\frac{9{t}^{5}}{5}-t){|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(-\\frac{9}{5}-1)-0 \\\\  & =-\\frac{14}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262740 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14506f23-b7d1-50e2-b7d6-575d362006d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260054} {:answer/id #uuid "c9aa7ce3-f49a-53f5-8930-c62b6d6c8760" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261659} {:answer/id #uuid "ecd35a62-5758-5dfb-8a79-58eaad86d417" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261954} {:answer/id #uuid "f5c6fb02-0c1a-52ca-8814-4034572247cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262025} {:answer/id #uuid "fde5bf23-2aa5-5f61-aed8-c77dfd32c840" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262101}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f5c6fb02-0c1a-52ca-8814-4034572247cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262025} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ffccb8a-a39d-5a7a-a89e-228e3ce4f2c6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262209}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "42dae85e-af3c-52f4-a342-edbfb5509ac9" :question/math-academy-id "q-127253" :question/problem "![](images/cd/cd21413d8eea5e2fec67eebe22967c846fd7916fd4fb0fbe5f8efe7ac9df6b1a.png)\n\nThe curve $C$ is given by $y=1-x$ from the point $(0,1)$ to the point $(1,0).$ Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=\\,i+\\,j$ through $C,$ measured with respect to the unit normal vector $n$ on $C,$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ measured with respect to a unit normal vector $n$ on $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo apply this formula, \n\n- the curve should be parameterized as $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and\n\n- the unit normal vector $n$ *must* be a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of the unit tangent vector $T(t).$\n\nParameterizing the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=t,\\,y=1-t,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\n \n\nThis parameterization traverses the curve from the point $(0,1)$ to the point $(1,0).$ Therefore, $n$ is indeed a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of $T,$ so we can apply the above formula for the flux. \n\n![](images/e2/e288ed1095be61e4645fb63c22b7fd2cb605d34795c30557e84afb31b7ec8721.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =1, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)=1, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-t)=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(1)\\cdot (-1)-(1)\\cdot (1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}-2\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(-2t){|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-2-0 \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262741 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "62235db6-575c-58cc-9ea4-bb528398093e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260721} {:answer/id #uuid "7a8d5f64-67d3-5dce-8551-9f910cdef724" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260944} {:answer/id #uuid "dc023b88-e9da-5465-abd7-a53791e497db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261823} {:answer/id #uuid "edf8fb76-10ab-53d9-a7da-5af08b0f7758" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261967} {:answer/id #uuid "faea5b08-5f3d-5e20-a75e-e18c259cc4db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262070}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "edf8fb76-10ab-53d9-a7da-5af08b0f7758" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261967} :answer-field/id #uuid "afced7f6-385b-5b50-b3de-6f71c6df96dc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262439}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6e7eff5f-8b84-5752-bf2b-9206857b0d37" :question/math-academy-id "q-127254" :question/problem "![](images/67/67dca8f0202d0c5d1041935a6aee7c4ebd0e3c6c449f2ee1a5ff24c1f381410c.png)\n\nThe curve $C$ is given by $x=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$ from the point $(1,\\frac{1}{e})$ to the point $(0,1).$ Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=2{y}^{2}\\,j$ through $C,$ measured with respect to the unit normal vector $n$ on $C,$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ measured with respect to a unit normal vector $n$ on $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo apply this formula, \n\n- the curve should be parameterized as $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and\n\n- the unit normal vector $n$ *must* be a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of the unit tangent vector $T(t).$\n\nParameterizing the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\,y=t,\\,t\\in [\\frac{1}{e},1].\n$$\n\n \n\nThis parameterization traverses the curve from the point $(1,\\frac{1}{e})$ to the point $(0,1).$ Therefore, $n$ is indeed a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of $T,$ so we can apply the above formula for the flux. \n\n![](images/08/08fc4561a322b690da7bd28a9e69a91c687256e8a902ec7690dda5e6a1c689a6.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =0, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =2{y}^{2}=2{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)=-\\frac{1}{t}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/e}^{1}(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/e}^{1}(0)\\cdot (1)-(2{t}^{2})\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{t})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/e}^{1}2t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =({t}^{2}){|}_{1/e}^{1} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362552 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d3de62fa-1c74-52d8-bd89-6f7ea249f514" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362554}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d3de62fa-1c74-52d8-bd89-6f7ea249f514" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362554} :answer-field/id #uuid "674f2487-9757-5196-815f-fe7a2e6b0fcb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362553}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c4eeab3d-e63c-5f1b-81ca-c4c4901eb313" :question/math-academy-id "q-169206" :question/problem "![](images/cf/cfbc570ba4fc6bd4923edc2d0efae0e735e671f9b4350226159e591e0bdae6c7.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\n                            The net $L$ is represented by the line segment shown in the diagram above. \nCalculate the flux of $F$ through $L,$ measured with respect to the unit normal vector $n.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${\\int }_{L}^{}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s\\,=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ measured with respect to a unit normal vector $n$ on $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo apply this formula, \n\n* the curve should be parameterized as $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and\n\n* the unit normal vector $n$ *must* be a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of the unit tangent vector $T(t).$\n\nParameterizing the line segment $L,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{2}t,\\,y=-\\sqrt{2}t,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\n \n\nThis parameterization traverses the line from the point $(0,0)$ to the point $(\\sqrt{2},-\\sqrt{2}).$ Therefore, $n$ is indeed a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of $T,$ so we can apply the above formula for the flux. \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =3x{y}^{2} \\\\  & =3(\\sqrt{2}t)(-\\sqrt{2}t)^{2} \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{2}{t}^{3}, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =-{y}^{3} \\\\  & =-(-\\sqrt{2}t)^{3} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}{t}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{2}t)=\\sqrt{2}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\sqrt{2}t)=-\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{L}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{L}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(6\\sqrt{2}{t}^{3})\\cdot (-\\sqrt{2})-(2\\sqrt{2}{t}^{3})\\cdot (\\sqrt{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-12{t}^{3}-4{t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}-16{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-4[{t}^{4}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-4(1-0) \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421761 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54352619-e7d8-51ab-bf81-2466d57fb916" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421740} {:answer/id #uuid "5f942441-ff7e-546f-8b6c-c0bc3ed8b008" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421741} {:answer/id #uuid "6097d82c-a160-580e-ba98-359ee8f02940" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421742} {:answer/id #uuid "8ebff72e-dab2-5efc-93c1-8a98f5c588a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421744} {:answer/id #uuid "a5ebd4b0-5c2f-5c66-823d-14cb8a9a334c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421747}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6097d82c-a160-580e-ba98-359ee8f02940" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421742} :answer-field/id #uuid "04fdbb04-1f5e-524e-9c27-6e5e40173080" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421755}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "735394db-bfa6-5cb2-9d7d-19fe3d01e994" :question/math-academy-id "q-127256" :question/problem "![](images/62/62c8f6d7db345ce76bd164bb52c13e5edef9c04f18f4f07501c8694e7f64b554.png)\n\nThe curve $C$ is given by $x={e}^{y}$ from the point $(1,0)$ to the point $(e,1).$ Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)={y}^{2}\\,i+\\frac{1}{x}\\,j$ through $C,$ measured with respect to the unit normal vector $n$ on $C,$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ measured with respect to a unit normal vector $n$ on $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo apply this formula, \n\n- the curve should be parameterized as $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and\n\n- the unit normal vector $n$ *must* be a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of the unit tangent vector $T(t).$\n\nParameterizing the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx={e}^{t}\\,y=t,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\n \n\nThis parameterization traverses the curve from the point $(1,0)$ to the point $(e,1).$ Therefore, $n$ is indeed a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of $T,$ so we can apply the above formula for the flux. \n\n![](images/ba/ba6285008fd90835fdbf73203b76a0a01d713889d9939979aaec54bebd0a8c49.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & ={y}^{2}={t}^{2}, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{x}={e}^{-t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})={e}^{t}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2})\\cdot (1)-({e}^{-t})\\cdot ({e}^{t})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}-1\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}-t){|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{3}-1)-0 \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421764 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a0fbaf0-fbaf-5ffb-ba78-e300b9c3cb2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421731} {:answer/id #uuid "527da9e8-31b2-5d12-ada1-f6448689e756" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421739} {:answer/id #uuid "b79b1436-2e95-5a91-a8c3-0d137af242fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421748} {:answer/id #uuid "d6be670d-4038-5dbc-ac98-7208e5a8c53f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421751} {:answer/id #uuid "dfbe47bf-91a3-5026-b0f2-e265e56e1b7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421752}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0a0fbaf0-fbaf-5ffb-ba78-e300b9c3cb2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421731} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f7eefb0-93b0-549d-9805-f25d2ffc89e0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421757}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ddff35a9-8792-529b-9827-d26481dbe529" :question/math-academy-id "q-127235" :question/problem "![](images/95/95e6a455863f0b4cefe184cca9ad86feaeff9f0702808db2d151f5d6615a04b8.png)\n\nThe curve $C$ is given by $y=\\frac{1}{x}$ from the point $(\\frac{1}{2},2)$ to the point $(2,\\frac{1}{2}).$ Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)={x}^{2}y\\,i-y\\,j$ through $C,$ measured with respect to the unit normal vector $n$ on $C,$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ measured with respect to a unit normal vector $n$ on $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo apply this formula, \n\n- the curve should be parameterized as $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and\n\n- the unit normal vector $n$ *must* be a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of the unit tangent vector $T(t).$\n\nParameterizing the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=t,\\,y=\\frac{1}{t},\\,t\\in [\\frac{1}{2},2].\n$$\n\n \n\nThis parameterization traverses the curve from the point $(\\frac{1}{2},2)$ to the point $(2,\\frac{1}{2}).$ Therefore, $n$ is indeed a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of $T,$ so we can apply the above formula for the flux. \n\n![](images/cc/cc9d66f800a57f27673577cd3fd179b4cd6ff64585c5dc1a867317d8d592e485.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & ={x}^{2}y=t, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =-y=-\\frac{1}{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)=1, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{t})=-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{2}(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{2}(t)\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}})-(-\\frac{1}{t})\\cdot (1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{2}0\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Flux of a Vector Field Over a Non-Closed Curve"}]}
